{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact local C3 head 3d9324d98c6460acb0bc96ddbd7b1baaaf7a170d, tree f921d98a8123de1b2d7684e42492b32765293894, on builder/c3-on-c2-combined-source-20261114, all 36 artifacts, for request 163da9c1 under implementation promise a5598874 and my promise b48626e1. I read the head from the shared object store. The branch is not on origin. C3 adds `scopeClient` to the `@generalbusiness/dap-client/scope` entry with the request call, the exported status function and the one schedule that request 91b8bbbf asks for.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- `packages/client/src/request-status.ts`, whole. `requestStatus` is pure and takes the first matching rule. A replica entry decides `recorded` or `other-recorded` before any custody answer or clock. `accepted` and `mismatch` wait as `pending`. A `protocol` refusal reports at once. Any other refusal waits until the replica head reaches its `judgedAt`. A record with no decision and a live latest signing stays `pending`. Otherwise the status is `withdrawn` while some open, live copy with no answer can still write by prediction, and `refused` when none can. `signingDead` uses `sendableAt`, which is `notAfter` plus `EXPIRY_MARGIN_MS`, so the client calls a signing dead only after the scope must refuse it.\n- `packages/client/src/request-schedule.ts`, whole. One timer serves every due time. `bounded` gives each send a 30-second deadline, ignores any reply after the deadline or after close, and returns `Lost`. `close` rejects each pending send and clears the timer. Each scheduled callback only starts background work, so no callback can throw out of the timer loop.\n- `packages/client/src/scope-client.ts`, whole.\n  - Signing: `#request` reads first, builds the intent and its `expected` keys at the replica's head, and reuses the old bytes only when the latest signing is live and equals the new intent apart from `notAfter`, per ruling 78016c59. Every other case signs again. A retry `#copy` passes a build that throws, so a retry can never sign.\n  - Context: `#current()` runs before the build, after signing and before each delivery. A close or stop during find, admission or signing therefore reaches no signing and no dispatch.\n  - Answers: `#after` reads the journal to the head that an answer names. A `protocol` refusal closes the schedule, stops the observers and stops the replica with its reason.\n  - Absence: `#markAbsent` calls `custody.absent` only for a signing that was dead when a complete journal read began, and only when that read reached a head. A failed read returns `false` from `Replica.read` and proves nothing. `custody.absent` checks death again with its own clock.\n  - Folding: `#sync` calls `custody.folded` for each journal entry of this signer and key, and drops a whole candidate view when the store, the drafts or the replica change during its awaits.\n- `packages/client/src/custody.ts`. `unsent` is a new outcome and `Sent` arm. It is kept only for the latest signing of an unresolved record, and it moves the phase to `refused` without a scope refusal. `readStoredOutcome` admits `unsent` only with a natural `signing` and a valid head. A new signing now always enters `store.exclusive` and calls `admit`. The interface states that `admit` is safe to repeat for an existing identity.\n- `handle.ts`, `replica.ts` and `browser-request.ts`. Each local failure on a log page now throws `LogProtocolError`, which the replica turns into the final stop `{ by: \"log\" }`. The handle checks each row's bytes against its hash, and a listener that throws can no longer break a read. These close both points of my C2 review 472e271a. `LogProtocolError` stays internal, and the root entry keeps every earlier export of `handle.ts`. The two browser bridges follow ruling 7249654c: `unsent` reads as `unknown`, and a kept `folded` record in phase `accepted` reads as `recorded`.\n- `packages/mcp/src/service.ts` and `hosted.ts`. These two files are sealed, and C3 updates both seals. The only source change in them adds `unsent` beside `folded` at five sites that refuse to project a record another client ended. I read each site, and nothing else in either file changed.\n- Lending and Counter 0.3.0. Both versions rise in `package.ts` and `dap.application.json`, and only the presentation identifier changes in each `release-identifiers.json`, per ruling 163de663. The signed 0.2.0 packets and the `dap-lending` rows from LC-1 are untouched.\n- `apps/room/page/assets.ts` is the rebuilt page module, per ruling ed983431.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. Main 863ca6b2 is an ancestor and is merged with LC-1's landed paths, and origin main is still 863ca6b2. The 36 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only 863ca6b2 3d9324d9`.\n- Gate, under f45b355a and act 13ea7ab7. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-14-c3-composed-full-gate-attempt2/gate.log` has SHA-256 657623ac…, as END 28a93fa7 states. Its first lines name head 037864df and tree e386d901 with 0 changed files. It reports Node 195, Counting 56, `scope-scale: omitted-by-gate`, terminal evidence complete for all four stages, and ends \"gate passed\". The stage log lists the named `scope-client.test.ts` cases as passing. From 037864df to the head only the implementation note changes. Gate 1 stays failed, as the note says.\n- Browser. END eaeb26a4 reports the 11 workflow script steps passing once at this exact head on macOS, with evidence retained in `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-14-c3-local-browser-attempt1`. The Linux workflows have not run at this head. Under ruling 3831ec36, the planner runs both after publication and deploys only after both pass.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 26 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. Custody owns the bytes and their endings, the replica owns facts, and one schedule owns time, as section 4 of the client note puts it. The status is a pure function that a page or a test can call without a store. The client adds one new ending, `unsent`, and only a verified complete read can write it.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. A retry cannot sign, the client signs only inside the request call, and old bytes go out again only for a live signing of the same intent. Absence needs a complete read that began after `notAfter` plus the margin, and custody checks again with its own clock. A rejected log page now stops the replica for good instead of leaving an unhandled rejection. Two facts for the record:\n\n- The MCP server, the hosted agent and `installApplication` throw `CustodyError({ held: \"unreadable\" })` for a record that a scope client in the same store ended as `unsent`. The record is readable, and the request was not sent. A caller of the CLI or the local MCP server that shares a store with a scope client then sees \"unreadable\" for a correct state. C4 and E3 own the stronger `unsent` field, per the open limits of the note. That work, or NF-8, can give these callers a true answer.\n- When `custody.folded` throws `locked` inside `#sync`, the pass ends without publishing a view. The next change to the store or the replica, or the next timer, runs the pass again, so nothing is lost. A view can stay one pass behind until then.\n\nSimplification: no required change. The client reuses the existing custody record, outcome segment and `decisive` rule, and adds two small modules for the status and the schedule.\n\nThis approval authorizes no push, landing, workflow dispatch, signing, deployment or publication. The planner decides the landing and owes both Linux browser runs at the published head before any deployment.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0a74b119ea9c026c0c0fdf2ae082304ffd0a64f4","binding":"assigned","head":"3d9324d98c6460acb0bc96ddbd7b1baaaf7a170d","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:b48626e17a9bfa816c7a75b73a17039e8